/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  batch14_nacl_pitzer_antisolvent  --  a batch crystalliser whose charge holds
                                       an ANTISOLVENT its model cannot use.

  The sibling of batch13, one component apart.  batch13 is water + NaCl under
  a Pitzer package; this case adds ethanol to the same charge and changes
  nothing else.  A reader expects the ethanol to lower the solubility --- that
  is what an antisolvent is for --- and the run says, in the engine's own
  words, that it does not:

      [drowningOut] m_sat is UNCHANGED from the aqueous datum: this model
                    carries no mixed-solvent term ...

  Pitzer here is the pairwise aqueous form: its parameters are regressed on
  water, and there is no term in it that knows a second solvent exists.  The
  ethanol is therefore REAL in the material balance and ABSENT from the
  saturation, and the honest thing for the run to do is say so rather than
  print a drowning-out that drowns nothing.  Its eNRTL sibling
  (crystalliser06, steady) does move m_sat, 6.14 -> 0.885 mol/kg, so the two
  side by side show what "the antisolvent effect" costs: not a component in
  the list, a term in the model.

  The second announcement is about the reference the saturation is solved
  against.  Whatever gamma_pm does with the mixed solvent, the equilibrium
  constant stays referenced to infinite dilution in PURE WATER --- the
  standard-state transfer term is the named next slice (D3), unimplemented,
  and the run states it instead of leaving it at zero in silence.

  Both lines come from the SHARED saturation resolver, not from this unit:
  the steady crystalliser prints the identical pair.  A gap that belongs to a
  number should not depend on who prints it.

  This case also fixed a defect it was built to exercise.  The batch
  supersaturation divided the dissolved salt by the WATER mass while c_sat
  came back on the resolver's mixed-solvent basis (water + ethanol) --- two
  different denominators, one ratio, no complaint from either side.  With the
  charge below it read S0 = 1.592 instead of 1.240 --- a 28 % overstatement of
  the one number the whole population balance turns on, in a model where the
  nucleation rate goes as a power of (S-1).
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

application   choupoBatch;
description   "Batch NaCl crystalliser with ethanol present: a pairwise-aqueous model announces the mixed-solvent term it does not carry";

verbosity     3;

startTime       0;        // s
endTime         7200;     // s   (2 h)
deltaT          2.0;      // s   (RK4 step)
writeInterval   360;      // s
